      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 Authors; published by Portland Press Limited.",
        "AbstractText": "Null mutations of the Niemann-Pick type C1 (NPC1) gene cause NPC disease, a lysosomal storage disorder characterized by cholesterol accumulation in late endosomes (LE) and lysosomes (Ly). Nascent or mutated NPC1 is degraded through the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway, but how NPC1 degradation is regulated remains currently unknown. In the present study, we demonstrated a link between NPC1 degradation and the Akt (protein kinase B)/mTOR [mammalian (or mechanistic) target of rapamycin] signalling pathway in cervical cancer cell lines. We provided evidence that activated Akt/mTOR pathway increased NPC1 degradation by ∼50% in C33A cells when compared with SiHa or HeLa cells. NPC1 degradation in C33A cells was reversed when Akt/mTOR activation was blocked by specific inhibitors or when mTORC1 (mTOR complex 1) was disrupted by regulatory associated protein of mTOR (Raptor) knockdown. Importantly, inhibition of the Akt/mTOR pathway led to decreased NPC1 ubiquitination in C33A cells, pointing to a role of Akt/mTOR in the proteasomal degradation of NPC1. Moreover, we found that NPC1 depletion in several cancer cell lines inhibited cell proliferation and migration. Our results uncover Akt as a key regulator of NPC1 degradation and link NPC1 to cancer cell proliferation and migration."
      },
